fact WorkItem(id: "WI-951", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND'S WI-850 TYPE-PARAM-DEFAULT REFUSAL QUOTES RAW CST TEXT, so a default written with an interior comment or line break renders that trivia into the diagnostic. The scaland twin was fixed under WI-950 (2026-08-03); the rust side is untouched and is the reason this is a ticket. MECHANISM: `convert_operation_type_param` (rustland/anthill-core/src/parse/convert.rs, ~3485) takes `let written = self.text(default)`, and `text` is a raw byte slice over `source[node.start_byte()..node.end_byte()]`. Comments are tree-sitter `extras` (grammar.js lists whitespace, `line_comment`, `block_comment`), so anything written INSIDE the type node sits inside that byte range. MEASURED 2026-08-03 by a scratch test on `wi_tests`: an `operation foo[T = List[ {- note -} Int64` continued on the next line as `]](x: T) -> T` produces a message quoting the default as `List[ {- note -} Int64` plus the line break and indentation before `]` — and the same blob a SECOND time, in the suggested call-site spelling `foo[T = …](…)`. What is already CORRECT and needs no work: a TRAILING comment (`[T = Int64 -- why` then a newline then `]`) renders clean, because it falls outside the type node's byte range; only trivia INTERIOR to the type is affected. FIX, with scaland's shape for reference: WI-950 added `normalizeCapture` — every run of trivia collapses to one space, and a string literal is copied verbatim so a comment opener inside one (`Tagged[\"a -- b\"]`) is not eaten as trivia — sharing one trivia definition with the whitespace instance, so the parser cannot skip a comment form that the diagnostic then renders. rustland can do better than a slice scan: walk the `default` node's children and skip `line_comment` / `block_comment` nodes, or render the converted `TypeExpr`. NOTE this is a DIAGNOSTIC-TEXT defect only — the refusal itself fires correctly and WI-850's acceptance tests pass; the cost is that the author reads their own comment back twice inside a message about a dropped default. ACCEPTANCE: a test asserting the quoted default for a type written with an interior block comment and an interior line break (the measured repro above), plus one whose default contains a string literal holding a comment opener, so the normalisation cannot eat it. Both must FAIL on today's rustland."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-951", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the \"FIX, with scaland's shape for reference\" paragraph — do NOT copy what that paragraph describes. The first cut of the WI-950 fix normalised the `.!` source capture by re-lexing it for trivia, and /code-review measured that this TRUNCATES a `-`-bearing identifier: an anthill identifier is `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*`, so `my--type` is ONE token whose `--` is not a line comment, and the refusal quoted (and then RECOMMENDED) `T = my`. Stripping trivia from a capture needs the whole token model, not just the comment forms. The delivered scaland fix instead renders the parsed `TypeExpr` (`renderTypeExpr` / `renderTerm` / `renderLiteral` in AnthillParser.scala), whose matches are exhaustive over sealed types — a new IR case is a compile error rather than a silently mis-rendered diagnostic. So for rustland, take the route this ticket already lists second: render the converted `TypeExpr`, or walk the `default` node's children skipping `line_comment` / `block_comment` nodes. ADD to the acceptance criteria: a default written as a hyphenated identifier (`[T = my--type]`) must be quoted back in full — that case passes on today's rustland (it quotes raw text) and is the one a re-lexing fix would break, so it belongs in the suite as a control.", at: "2026-08-03T07:21:36Z")
